  "narrative": {
    "intro": "FY2027 to date federal contract obligations in West Virginia total $78.5M. Obligations fell 95.7% year over year. The state recorded 667 awards with an average award size of $118K. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer, obligating $46.5M so far, followed by the Department of Justice with $13.3M and the Department of the Treasury with $4.9M. Ambulance Services lead industry spending at $12.6M, and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction follows with $10M. Top vendors include HAMILTON PACIFIC CHAMBERLAIN LLC at $4M and AAECON GENERAL CONTRACTING LLC at $3.8M. FY2027 totals are partial and reflect the fiscal year to date rather than a full fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the FY2027 to date drop in federal obligations?",
        "body": "State-level obligations are $78.5M in FY2027 to date, down 95.7% year over year. Award activity shows 667 contracts with an average size of $118K, indicating many smaller awards. Historical context shows prior fiscal years ranged from $1.5B to $2.2B, which highlights how current-year totals are partial. The Department of Veterans Affairs accounts for the largest share of remaining FY2027 spending at $46.5M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors captured most FY2027 federal spending in West Virginia?",
        "body": "Ambulance Services are the largest industry with $12.6M, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $10M and Engineering Services at $8.1M. HAMILTON PACIFIC CHAMBERLAIN LLC leads vendors with $4M, AAECON GENERAL CONTRACTING LLC and JAN-CARE AMBULANCE OF RALEIGH COUNTY, INC. follow with $3.8M each. Other notable vendors include NEXTGEN FEDERAL SYSTEMS LLC at $3.7M and RAPID EMS LLC at $3.2M. Several medical and construction-related industries show concentrated spending in the FY2027 to date totals."
      }
    ]
  },
